From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 07:58:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Reset databases stored with another schema version
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The IndexedDB database files carried no version, so a later change to
the tables, or to how records are stored in them, could not tell an old
database from a current one. The rule for this fork is to delete and
recreate such a database rather than migrate it.

schema.rs now has SCHEMA_VERSION, 1, which the transaction that creates
the tables also stores in the file's `PRAGMA user_version`.
`schema::open_database` opens the database and reads that value:

- 0 and no tables: a new file, which gets its tables;
- SCHEMA_VERSION: kept as it is;
- anything else, including 0 with tables, which every database created
  before this change has: deleted with all its files and created anew.

The IndexedDB version in the `database` table is a separate value that
this doesn't touch. `SqlEngine::new` reports a recreated database as
created, so that `open()` without a version upgrades it to 1, as it does
for a new one. Every later change to the tables or to the record format
bumps SCHEMA_VERSION.

`SqlExecutor::delete` closes the connection and deletes the database.
The SQLite executor closes it and removes the -wal, -shm and -journal
files, then the database file. The RDB executor drops its handles and
calls OH_Rdb_DeleteStoreV2 (API 14), which also removes RDB's -dwr and
-compare files and closes the store in RDB's store cache; it refuses to
while other executors on the store exist, which RDB would close the
store under. OH_Rdb_DeleteStoreV2 reports success even when it couldn't
remove the files, so after reopening, `open_database` checks that the
database is new, and fails the open otherwise rather than running the
current code on an old database. The RDB store configuration moved into
`OhosRdbConfig::for_store`, which open and delete share.

On the DAYU200, user_version behaves as in plain SQLite across RDB's
connection pool: set in a transaction, it is rolled back with it;
committed, the store's other connections see it, and so does a reopened
store, and OH_Rdb_GetVersion returns the same value. RDB opens NDK
stores with version -1, so it never writes user_version itself.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 78 passed (71 before; the 7 new tests below). DAYU200 (`OHOS_TEST_RUNNER_HDC_TARGET=… CARGO_TARGET_AARCH64_UNKNOWN_LINUX_OHOS_RUNNER=ohos-test-runner ./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`): RDB only 77 passed, both backends 126 passed (one documented skip). The new tests: a new database is stamped; a matching one is kept with its stores and version; one stored with version 0 (the state before this change) or SCHEMA_VERSION + 1 is recreated, without a table that was added to the old file; a reset whose delete leaves the old file fails the open, and the next open resets; an IndexedDB version of 2^53 - 1 leaves the schema version alone; user_version is transactional and seen by other connections and after reopening; delete removes the database, also with a transaction open; on RDB, delete refuses while another executor on the store exists. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free in the three configurations. WPT IndexedDB/ with the whole P0 series applied, as measured for "indexeddb: Run the SQLite and RDB backends as executors of one engine": host SpiderMonkey 3,224, host QuickJS 3,229 and DAYU200 3,222 passing subtests of 4,511, every unexpected result also seen with the baseline build; idbtransaction-oncomplete 60 of 60.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and its tests, checked user_version and store deletion on the DAYU200, and ran the host and device tests and WPT; after a review, it made a reset that leaves the old file fail the open, made the RDB delete refuse while other executors exist, and measured again)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
